---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import katex from "katex";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/")
  ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL
  : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;

type Cell = [number, number];

/** A 6 by 6 diagram in the paper's idiom: black stones ours, white the
 * opponent's, dashed cells the threats, letters for labels. */
function board6(opts: {
  black?: Cell[];
  white?: Cell[];
  threats?: Cell[];
  labels?: [number, number, string][];
  squares?: [number, number, number][];
}): string {
  const U = 24;
  const size = 6 * U;
  const parts: string[] = [];
  parts.push(
    `<rect width="${size}" height="${size}" fill="url(#grid6p)"></rect>`,
    `<rect width="${size}" height="${size}" fill="none" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="0.8" opacity="0.5"></rect>`,
  );
  for (const [r, c, s] of opts.squares ?? []) {
    parts.push(
      `<rect x="${c * U + 3}" y="${r * U + 3}" width="${(s + 1) * U - 6}" height="${(s + 1) * U - 6}" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"></rect>`,
    );
  }
  for (const [r, c] of opts.black ?? []) {
    parts.push(
      `<circle cx="${c * U + 12}" cy="${r * U + 12}" r="6.5" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>`,
    );
  }
  for (const [r, c] of opts.white ?? []) {
    parts.push(
      `<circle cx="${c * U + 12}" cy="${r * U + 12}" r="6.5" fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.3"></circle>`,
    );
  }
  for (const [r, c] of opts.threats ?? []) {
    parts.push(
      `<circle cx="${c * U + 12}" cy="${r * U + 12}" r="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 2.5"></circle>`,
    );
  }
  for (const [r, c, text] of opts.labels ?? []) {
    parts.push(
      `<text x="${c * U + 12}" y="${r * U + 17}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-style="italic" fill="var(--ink)">${text}</text>`,
    );
  }
  return parts.join("");
}

const anatomy1 = board6({
  labels: [
    [2, 2, "c"],
    [1, 2, "a"],
    [3, 2, "a"],
    [2, 1, "a"],
    [2, 3, "a"],
    [1, 1, "d"],
    [1, 3, "d"],
    [3, 1, "d"],
    [3, 3, "d"],
  ],
  squares: [[1, 1, 1]],
});
const anatomy2 = board6({
  labels: [
    [3, 2, "c"],
    [1, 2, "a"],
    [5, 2, "a"],
    [3, 0, "a"],
    [3, 4, "a"],
    [1, 0, "d"],
    [1, 4, "d"],
    [5, 0, "d"],
    [5, 4, "d"],
  ],
  squares: [[1, 0, 2]],
});
const basic = board6({
  black: [
    [2, 2],
    [3, 2],
    [2, 1],
    [1, 2],
    [2, 3],
  ],
  white: [
    [0, 0],
    [4, 4],
    [3, 1],
    [1, 1],
  ],
  threats: [
    [1, 3],
    [3, 3],
  ],
});
const tempoEnd = board6({
  black: [
    [2, 2],
    [3, 2],
    [3, 3],
    [4, 3],
    [3, 4],
    [1, 2],
    [5, 2],
    [3, 0],
  ],
  white: [
    [0, 0],
    [3, 1],
    [2, 3],
    [4, 2],
    [4, 4],
    [1, 4],
    [5, 4],
  ],
  threats: [
    [1, 0],
    [5, 0],
  ],
});
---

<Base
  title="How the proof works · A short winning rule"
  description="An illustrated guide to the plus rule: a winning strategy for the square achievement game on every board from 6 by 6 up, stated without a lookup table, verified by exhaustive search and formalized in Lean."
>
  <header class="masthead">
    <h1>How the proof works</h1>
    <p class="byline">An illustrated guide to the plus rule</p>
  </header>

  <p class="abstract">
    <strong>Summary.</strong> Two players alternately claim cells of an
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid; the first to hold all four corners
    of an axis-aligned square wins. The threshold result gave a first-player strategy
    for every board from 6 × 6 up, driven by a table of some eight thousand forcing
    plans. This result replaces the table by a rule built on one shape, the plus,
    with at most three preparatory forcing moves. The rule is verified by exhaustive
    search on the boards from 6 × 6 to 17 × 17, a run on the 23 × 23 board is carried
    to every larger board, and the whole argument is formalized in Lean 4 as a theorem
    about the rule's own play.
  </p>

  <figure class="game">
    <sag-board n="6" strategy="plus"></sag-board>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 1. The bot follows the rule described below and explains each move.
      Change n to explore.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Pluses</h2>
  <p>
    A <em>plus</em> of side <span set:html={k("s")} /> is a centre cell with its four
    <em>arms</em>, the cells at distance <span set:html={k("s")} /> above, below,
    left, and right, and its four <em>diagonals</em>, the cells at distance <span
      set:html={k("s")}
    /> in both directions. The centre, two perpendicular arms, and the diagonal between
    them are the four corners of a square of side <span set:html={k("s")} />. So
    once we hold the centre and one arm, each further arm perpendicular to a
    held arm makes three corners of such a square, and the opponent must block
    at the diagonal or lose; and once we hold two opposite arms, the next arm is
    perpendicular to both and threatens two diagonals at once, which no single
    block answers. Inside the 6 × 6 corner of the board there are sixteen pluses
    of side 1 and four of side 2; side 3 does not fit.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 316 152"
      role="img"
      aria-label="Two 6 by 6 grids: the plus of side 1 at (2,2) and the plus of side 2 at (3,2), with centre, arms, and diagonals labelled"
    >
      <defs>
        <pattern
          id="grid6p"
          width="24"
          height="24"
          patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
        >
          <path
            d="M24 0H0V24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="var(--rule)"
            stroke-width="0.8"
            opacity="0.5"></path>
        </pattern>
      </defs>
      <g set:html={anatomy1} />
      <g transform="translate(164 0)" set:html={anatomy2} />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 2. Left, the plus of side 1 at (2,2); right, the plus of side 2 at
      (3,2). The centre is c, the arms a, the diagonals d. The dashed outline
      encloses one of the four squares of each plus.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The opening (2,2) and the second move (3,2) are the centre and an arm of the
    side-1 plus at (2,2), and an arm and the centre of the side-1 plus at (3,2).
    Against most replies one of these pluses wins outright, in three moves.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 152 152"
      role="img"
      aria-label="The end of the basic line: black holds the plus of side 1 at (2,2) except the last arm's threats"
    >
      <g set:html={basic} />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 3. The basic line against the replies (0,0) and (4,4). The arms
      (2,1) and (1,2) each forced a block on a diagonal; the last arm (2,3)
      threatens at both dashed cells; the win comes on the eleventh ply.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Tempo moves and lines</h2>
  <p>
    When the opponent's stones spoil both pluses, sitting on an arm or a
    diagonal, the rule first plays <em>tempo moves</em>: single forcing moves,
    each threatening exactly one square, whose block leaves the opponent without
    a threat of their own. A tempo move and its block add one stone to each side
    and hand the move back to us, and our new stone is where we chose it. A
    <em>line</em> is a sequence of at most three tempo moves followed by the arms
    of one plus, each arm before the last forcing a block and the last making two
    threats. The rule prefers lines with fewer tempo moves, then fewer arms left to
    play, then the smaller plus, then the earliest cells, and it commits to the chosen
    line after the opponent's second reply. Every line has at most six moves, and
    the win comes by the seventeenth ply of the game at the latest.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 152 152"
      role="img"
      aria-label="The end of the longest line: two tempo moves prepared the plus of side 2 at (3,2)"
    >
      <g set:html={tempoEnd} />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 4. The longest line, against the replies (0,0) and (3,1). The tempo
      moves (3,3) and (4,3) were each blocked; then the arms (3,4), (1,2), (5,2)
      of the side-2 plus at (3,2) each forced a block, and the last arm (3,0)
      threatens at both dashed cells.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The latent corner</h2>
  <p>
    The rule reads only an abstraction of the position: our stones, which never
    leave the 6 × 6 corner, the opponent's stones inside the 17 × 17 base board,
    and one more cell. Two opponent stones far from the corner, each with a
    coordinate of 12 or more, can be two corners of a square whose third corner
    lies in the 6 × 6 region; we call that region cell their <em
      >latent corner</em
    >. If the opponent were ever forced to block there, they would hold three
    corners of that square and threaten at its fourth, which may lie beyond
    anything the rule looks at. So the rule never makes a threat whose only
    block is the latent corner. A containment lemma from the threshold result
    says this is all the far stones can do: two stones of the corner region lie
    in a common square only if that square fits inside the 12 × 12 board, so a
    far stone shares no square with two of the opponent's forced blocks, and a
    square through a far stone and one block has both free replies as its other
    corners.
  </p>

  <h2>What is checked by hand and what by machine</h2>
  <p>
    Why arms force, why the last arm doubles, and why the abstraction sees
    everything that matters are short arguments written out in the paper. That
    every reply pair has a line, and that following it wins against every reply,
    is a case analysis too large for a page and is done by a checker. The
    checker walks the <em>forced tree</em>: every reply at the first two levels,
    and below that only the replies that block a threat, since a reply that
    blocks nothing loses to the standing threat at once. At every node it
    evaluates the rule afresh; no table is consulted anywhere. The runs for the
    boards from 6 × 6 to 17 × 17 take from under a second to 75 seconds each.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rules are only as trustworthy as the programs that check them, so the rule
    exists three times, in Python, in Lean, and in TypeScript for the bot on
    this page. The Lean version is the one the proof cites, and its checker
    comes with a machine-checked soundness theorem. The three are compared on
    the same positions: the pipeline exports the line the rule commits to for
    every reply pair on the 6 × 6 board and for the anchored positions of the
    next section, and the TypeScript rule is replayed against that fixture in
    the site's tests.
  </p>

  <h2>Every board at once</h2>
  <p>
    Boards from 17 × 17 up are covered by the simulation argument of the
    threshold result: a checked run on a base board is replayed on any larger
    board, with replies inside the base board read verbatim and replies beyond
    it replaced by stand-in cells. For the rule the base board is 23 × 23, the
    smallest that holds the stand-ins, and three families of anchored runs, 176
    positions in all, cover the reply pairs with a latent corner, with the
    latent corner as an extra guard. Since the rule reads only the abstraction,
    and the stand-ins lie beyond the 17 × 17 board and share the latent corner
    with the stones they replace, the rule computes the same move on the real
    board and on the base board; this agreement lemma is what makes the replayed
    run the rule's own play rather than merely a win found by the checker.
  </p>

  <h2>What the formal theorem says</h2>
  <p>
    The threshold result proved <code>P1CanWin</code>: a winning strategy tree
    exists, namely one the checker found. This result proves more. The predicate
    <code>WinsWith σ n h</code> says that the game tree in which the first player
    plays the move of the strategy <code>σ</code> at every node after the history
    <code>h</code>, against every legal reply, ends in a first-player square on
    every branch. The theorem is
  </p>
  <pre
    class="lean"><code>theorem plus_wins_with {"{"}n : ℕ{"}"} (h : 6 ≤ n) :
    WinsWith plusMove n []</code></pre>
  <p>
    where <code>plusMove</code> is the rule as a Lean definition, 250 lines with no
    data beyond the seven exceptional second moves. Its axioms are the usual three
    of Lean plus the six evaluated certificates, the runs at 6 to 16, at 17, on the
    23 × 23 board, and the three anchored families, discharged by
    <code>native_decide</code>. Both artifacts are built from this repository on
    every deploy:
    <a href={`${base}downloads/plus.pdf`}>the paper (PDF)</a> and
    <a href={`${base}downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz`}
      >the Lean development (tar.gz)</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>How the rule was found</h2>
  <p>
    The old table had 8,383 entries but only 186 distinct plans, and nearly all
    of them begin with an arm of the side-1 plus at (2,2) or (3,2). The shortest
    plans are that plus; the long ones, replayed in the positions where they are
    used, turn out to force blocks so as to place a stone a later square needs,
    or to play the arms of the side-2 plus at (3,2) one at a time. A first
    attempt at a rule scored moves by how many threats they made available and
    failed on 31 reply pairs; asking instead which plus can be completed by
    force, after a few tempo moves, gave a rule that wins them all. Compiled
    back into a table, the rule needs 2,330 entries against 8,135 and plays the
    same 79 lines on every board from 6 × 6 to 17 × 17.
  </p>

  <h2>Open questions</h2>
  <p>
    The rule wins by ply 17; the least possible is 15 on the 6, 7, and 8 boards,
    and at most 15 up to 14. (Adler's searches report 13, but they score a
    double threat at the search horizon as a loss, two plies before the
    completing move; his own depth-11 runs find no win, and his certified tree
    for 6 × 6 has its deepest leaves at ply 15.) Is there a rule of comparable
    length that wins by ply 15 on every board, and what is the least ply for
    large boards? The second move has seven exceptions; is there an opening from
    which a rule of this kind needs none? And the rule does not apply to 5 × 5,
    where no purely forcing strategy from (2,2) exists; a short rule for that
    board is open.
  </p>

